, L1 or L2 passes through (n,1). This is reminiscent of constructing two lines that intersect each column at distinct points, covering all points of sums n and n+1.\n\nIs this possible? Let's try to find such lines.\n\nWe need to assign for each i a point (i, n+1-i) (sum n+1) or (i, n-i) (sum n). Let's denote the two lines as L1 and L2. For each i, we need to decide which point goes to L1 and which to L2. The points must be collinear on each line.\n\nThus we need to partition the set of points into two subsets, each of which lies on a line (not vertical/horizontal/slope -1). This is a combinatorial geometry problem.\n\nWe can try to find a pattern: maybe L1 is the line connecting (1,n) and (n,1). That line has slope (1-n)/(n-1) = -(n-1)/(n-1) = -1. That's slope -1, which is not allowed for sunny lines. So that line is non-sunny. So we cannot use that.\n\nMaybe L1 is the line connecting (1,n) and (2,n-1). That slope = (n-1 - n)/(2-1) = -1/1 = -1, again not allowed. So any line connecting two points with sum n+1 that are adjacent columns will have slope -1. Actually any two points (i, n+1-i) and (j, n+1-j) have slope = ((n+1-j) - (n+1-i))/(j-i) = (i-j)/(j-i) = -1. So any line connecting two points from the same sum n+1 is slope -1. So we cannot have a sunny line covering two points from sum n+1. Similarly, any line connecting two points from sum n (i, n-i) and (j, n-j) has slope = ((n-j)-(n-i))/(j-i) = (i-j)/(j-i) = -1. So any line connecting two points from the same sum is slope -1, not allowed. So each sunny line can contain at most one point from each sum set. So each sunny line can contain at most one point from sum n and at most one point from sum n+1. Actually it can contain at most one point from each column, but also cannot contain two points from same sum because that would be slope -1. So each sunny line can contain at most one point from each column, and also at most one point from each sum. So each sunny line can contain at most one point from each column,